/*!
\page android-getting-started.html
\title Getting Started with Qt for Android
\brief A beginner's guide to install Qt and the Android toolchain and create
your first Qt for Android app with Qt Creator IDE.

This tutorial describes how to use \QC to develop a Qt Quick application for
Android devices.

You develop a Qt Quick application that accelerates an SVG (Scalable Vector
Graphics) image based on changing accelerometer values.

\image {android/android-gettingstarted-project-result.webp}
        {Accelerate Bubble example}

\section1 Set up the development environment

To get started with Qt for Android:

\list 1
    \li Download and install Qt. To do this, follow the instructions on the
        \l{Get and Install Qt} page.
    \li Install all the necessary tools and configure the development
        environment as explained in \l {Qt for Android Prerequisites}.
\endlist

\section1 Create the project

\list 1

    \li Open \QC. Go to \uicontrol File > \uicontrol {New Project} >
        \uicontrol {Application (Qt)} > \uicontrol {Qt Quick Application}.

        \image {android/android-gettingstarted-create-quick-project.webp}
               {New Project dialog}

    \li Select \uicontrol {Choose...} to open the \uicontrol {Project Location}
        dialog.

    \li In \uicontrol Name, enter a name for the application.

        \image {android/android-gettingstarted-project-name-location.webp}
               {Project Location dialog}

    \li In \uicontrol {Create in}, enter the path for the project files. You can
        move project folders later without problems.

    \li Select \uicontrol Next (or \uicontrol Continue on \macos) to open the
        \uicontrol {Define Project Details} dialog.

        \image {android/android-gettingstarted-project-details.webp}
               {Define Project Details dialog}

    \li Select \uicontrol Next (or \uicontrol Continue on \macos) to open the
        \uicontrol {Kit Selection} dialog.

    \li Select \l{\QC: Kits}{kits} for the platforms that you want to build
        the application for.

        \image {android/android-gettingstarted-project-kit-selection.webp}
            {Selecting kits for the project}

        \note The list shows kits that you specify in \uicontrol {Preferences} >
        \uicontrol Kits.
        For more information, see \l {\QC: Add kits}{Add kits} and
        \l {\QC: Kits}{Managing kits}.

    \li Select \uicontrol Next (or \uicontrol Continue on \macos) to open the
        \uicontrol {Project Management} dialog.

    \li Review the project settings, and select \uicontrol{Finish}
        (or \uicontrol Done on \macos) to create the project.

        \image {android/android-gettingstarted-project-management.webp}
               {Project Management dialog}
\endlist

\section1 Add images as resources

The main view of the application displays an SVG bubble image that moves around
the screen when you tilt the device.

For the image to appear when you run the application, you must specify it as a
resource in the \c RESOURCES argument of the \c qt_add_qml_module() method in
the \c CMakeLists.txt file that the wizard created for you:

\snippet tutorials/android-app/CMakeLists.txt qml-module

\section1 Create the Accelbubble main view

\list 1

\li In the \uicontrol Projects view, select \c Main.qml. In the \c Main.qml
    file, replace the content with the following code:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-0
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-1
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-2
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-4
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-5

    This code changes the default \l Window properties and adds the recently
    added image to the view. You can use any other image or component instead.

\li Add custom properties to position the image in respect to the width and
    height of the main window:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-0
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-1
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-2
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-3
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-4
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-5

\li Add the following import statement:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml import-sensors

    It is required because we want to move the bubble based on
    Accelerometer sensor values.

\li Add the \l{Accelerometer} component with the necessary properties:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-0
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml import-sensors
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-1
    \dots
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-2
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-4
    \dots

\li Add the following JavaScript functions that calculate the x and y position
    of the bubble based on the current Accelerometer values:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-0
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml import-sensors
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-1
    \dots
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-3
    \dots
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-4
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml calc-functions
    \dots


\li Add the following JavaScript code for \c onReadingChanged signal of
    Accelerometer component to make the bubble move when the Accelerometer
    values change:

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-2
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml on-reading-changed
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml accelerometer-4

    You need to ensure that the position of the bubble is always within the
    bounds of the screen. If the Accelerometer returns \e {not a number} (NaN),
    the value is ignored and the bubble position is not updated.

\li Add \l SmoothedAnimation behavior on the \c x and \c y properties of the
    bubble to make its movement look smoother.

    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml create-image-2
    \dots
    \snippet tutorials/android-app/Main.qml smoothed-animation
    \dots
\endlist

\section1 Lock device orientation

By default, the display rotates when the device orientation changes between
portrait and landscape. This application looks better if you lock the screen
orientation to portrait mode.

On Android, you set the screen orientation in the \c AndroidManifest.xml file,
which you generate in \QC. To lock the orientation to portrait:

\list 1
    \li Go to \uicontrol {Tools} > \uicontrol {Android} >
        \uicontrol {Manifest XML Source}. \QC creates the file in an \e android
        directory inside your project directory and opens it in the manifest
        editor.

    \li In the manifest editor, select \uicontrol {XML Source} and set the
        \c android:screenOrientation attribute of the \c activity element to
        \c portrait.

        \code{xml}
        <?xml version='1.0'?>
        <manifest ...>
            <supports-screens.../>
            <!--%%INSERT_PERMISSIONS-->
            <!--%%INSERT_FEATURES-->
            <application ...>
                <activity ... android:screenOrientation="portrait">
            ...
        \endcode

        For more information, see
        \l{\QC: Edit Android manifest files}{Edit Android manifest files}.
\endlist

\section1 Add dependencies

The application uses the Qt Sensors and Qt SVG modules, so you should add them
as dependencies in the \c CMakeLists.txt file. To add the dependencies:

\list 1
    \li Add the \c Sensors and \c Svg components to the \c find_package
        function. This tells CMake to look up the Qt installation and import the
        modules:

        \snippet tutorials/android-app/CMakeLists.txt find-package

    \li Add the imported targets to the \c target_link_libraries function:

        \snippet tutorials/android-app/CMakeLists.txt link-libraries

        This tells CMake that the \c accelbubble executable uses the modules.
        CMake then passes the necessary arguments to the linker, and the
        appropriate include directories and compiler definitions to the C++
        compiler.

    \li To apply the configuration changes, go to \uicontrol Build and select
        \uicontrol {Run CMake}.
\endlist

For more information about the \c CMakeLists.txt file, see
\l{Getting started with CMake}.

\note If you have not installed all the Qt modules listed in \c find_package(),
\QC asks whether you want to install them with \QOI.

\section1 Run the application

You can now deploy the application to a real or virtual Android device.

If you use a real device:

\list 1
    \li Enable \e{USB Debugging} on the Android device.
    \li Connect the device to the development PC.
\endlist

If you have several devices connected, \QC allows you to select the device to
run the application on.

\image {android/android-gettingstarted-select-device.webp}
        {Chose the device to run the application on}

To run the application on the device, select \key {Ctrl+R} (\key {Cmd+R}).

Once the application started on the device, you can tilt the device to see the
bubble move around the screen. The bubble moves in the direction of the tilt,
and the speed of the movement depends on the tilt angle. The bubble moves
faster when you tilt the device more.

\image {android/android-getting-started-result-in-action.gif}
        {Running the application on an Android device}

\sa {Qt Academy - Getting Started with Qt for Android}{Qt Academy: Getting Started with Qt for Android},
{Design Qt Quick UIs}{How to: Design Qt Quick UIs}, {Qt Quick UI design},
{Designing Qt Quick UIs}

*/
